Your digest uses the boundary =_quoted_5e0a, so a reader that matches
it anywhere stops at the first quote of it. Three ways it shows up:

1. Mid-line, as in: the parts are split on --=_quoted_5e0a here.

2. At the start of a line, followed by words:
--=_quoted_5e0a is only a delimiter when nothing but spaces follow it.

3. As the start of a longer token:
--=_quoted_5e0aX is a different boundary altogether.

All three are body text. This sentence is the end of the message.

--=_quoted_5e0a
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii; name="delimiter-rule.txt"
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="delimiter-rule.txt"

RFC 2046 5.1.1: a delimiter is CRLF, two hyphens, the boundary, optional
linear whitespace, CRLF.

